diff --git a/experiments/gravity_rollout_factorial/README.md b/experiments/gravity_rollout_factorial/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..de9a52a
--- /dev/null
+++ b/experiments/gravity_rollout_factorial/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,81 @@
+# Gravity and rollout factorial
+
+This experiment estimates the separate effects of physical-parameter
+conditioning and autoregressive rollout training in SG-JEPA.
+
+The four training cells use the same ViT-Tiny encoder, GRU predictor, latent
+size, optimizer, data split, and 20-epoch schedule.
+
+| Cell | Gravity input during training and evaluation | World-model loss |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `correct_onestep` | The normalized episode gravity | One-step teacher-forced loss |
+| `correct_rollout` | The normalized episode gravity | Five-step autoregressive rollout loss |
+| `constant_onestep` | Constant normalized mean, `0` | One-step teacher-forced loss |
+| `constant_rollout` | Constant normalized mean, `0` | Five-step autoregressive rollout loss |
+
+Each cell trains once with world-model seed `42`. Each trained checkpoint gets
+five independent probes and evaluations with seeds `42`, `43`, `44`, `45`, and
+`46`. The primary evaluation uses the paper-scale Square dataset and the
+existing state-probe metric at horizons 1, 3, 5, 9, 20, 32, and 44.
+
+## Run on a CML submission node
+
+Set these paths before submitting jobs. Keep the dataset and outputs in
+`/cmlscratch/$USER`; keep the Python environment in a backed-up location if
+you need it after the scratch allocation expires.
+
+```bash
+export SGJEPA_ROOT="$PWD"
+export SGJEPA_PYTHON="$SGJEPA_ROOT/.venv-paper/bin/python"
+export SGJEPA_DATASET="/cmlscratch/$USER/sg-jepa-factorial/data/square"
+export SGJEPA_OUTPUT="/cmlscratch/$USER/sg-jepa-factorial/output"
+```
+
+Generate the shared dataset once:
+
+```bash
+sbatch --account=cml-furongh --qos=cml-high_long experiments/gravity_rollout_factorial/slurm/data.sbatch
+```
+
+After the data job passes, submit the resource-specific arrays. The normal
+split runs `correct_onestep` and `constant_rollout` on B300. It runs
+`correct_rollout` and `constant_onestep` on H200.
+
+For the current run, reuse the completed `correct_onestep/seed_42` checkpoint.
+The two B300 slots first ran `constant_onestep` and `correct_rollout`. Queue
+`constant_rollout` with a 12-hour limit when a B300 slot opens.
+
+```bash
+wm_onestep=$(sbatch --account=cml-furongh --qos=cml-high_long --export=ALL --parsable --time=12:00:00 --gres=gpu:b300-sxm:1 --array=2%1 experiments/gravity_rollout_factorial/slurm/world_model.sbatch)
+wm_rollout=$(sbatch --account=cml-furongh --qos=cml-high_long --export=ALL --parsable --time=36:00:00 --gres=gpu:b300-sxm:1 --array=1%1 experiments/gravity_rollout_factorial/slurm/world_model.sbatch)
+probe_onestep=$(sbatch --account=cml-furongh --qos=cml-high_long --export=ALL --parsable --time=1-00:00:00 --gres=gpu:b300-sxm:1 --array=10-14%8 --dependency=afterok:$wm_onestep experiments/gravity_rollout_factorial/slurm/probe.sbatch)
+probe_rollout=$(sbatch --account=cml-furongh --qos=cml-high_long --export=ALL --parsable --time=1-00:00:00 --gres=gpu:b300-sxm:1 --array=5-9%8 --dependency=afterok:$wm_rollout experiments/gravity_rollout_factorial/slurm/probe.sbatch)
+eval_onestep=$(sbatch --account=cml-furongh --qos=cml-high_long --export=ALL --parsable --time=1-00:00:00 --gres=gpu:b300-sxm:1 --array=10-14%8 --dependency=afterok:$probe_onestep experiments/gravity_rollout_factorial/slurm/evaluate.sbatch)
+eval_rollout=$(sbatch --account=cml-furongh --qos=cml-high_long --export=ALL --parsable --time=1-00:00:00 --gres=gpu:b300-sxm:1 --array=5-9%8 --dependency=afterok:$probe_rollout experiments/gravity_rollout_factorial/slurm/evaluate.sbatch)
+wm_constant_rollout=$(sbatch --account=cml-furongh --qos=cml-high_long --export=ALL --parsable --time=12:00:00 --gres=gpu:b300-sxm:1 --array=3%1 experiments/gravity_rollout_factorial/slurm/world_model.sbatch)
+probe_constant_rollout=$(sbatch --account=cml-furongh --qos=cml-high_long --export=ALL --parsable --time=1-00:00:00 --gres=gpu:b300-sxm:1 --array=15-19%8 --dependency=afterok:$wm_constant_rollout experiments/gravity_rollout_factorial/slurm/probe.sbatch)
+eval_constant_rollout=$(sbatch --account=cml-furongh --qos=cml-high_long --export=ALL --parsable --time=1-00:00:00 --gres=gpu:b300-sxm:1 --array=15-19%8 --dependency=afterok:$probe_constant_rollout experiments/gravity_rollout_factorial/slurm/evaluate.sbatch)
+printf 'world one-step=%s world rollout=%s world constant-rollout=%s probe one-step=%s probe rollout=%s probe constant-rollout=%s eval one-step=%s eval rollout=%s eval constant-rollout=%s\\n' "$wm_onestep" "$wm_rollout" "$wm_constant_rollout" "$probe_onestep" "$probe_rollout" "$probe_constant_rollout" "$eval_onestep" "$eval_rollout" "$eval_constant_rollout"
+```
+
+For a fresh run, the world-model array uses 4 entries, one per cell. In this
+resumed run, the completed `correct_onestep/seed_42` checkpoint is reused. The
+two world-model jobs above train the one-step `constant_onestep` cell and the
+five-step `correct_rollout` cell. The separate `constant_rollout` job uses the
+same B300 pool and a 12-hour limit. The probe and evaluation jobs above cover
+five downstream seeds for each new cell.
+Each later stage checks for the output from its previous stage before it starts.
+
+Monitor the jobs with:
+
+```bash
+squeue --user="$USER"
+sacct --starttime=today --format=JobID,JobName,State,Elapsed,MaxRSS,AllocTRES
+```
+
+## Interpret the result
+
+For each horizon, compare the four cell means. The rollout effect at a fixed
+gravity condition is `correct_rollout - correct_onestep` or
+`constant_rollout - constant_onestep`. The grounding effect at a fixed loss is
+`correct_onestep - constant_onestep` or
+`correct_rollout - constant_rollout`.
+
+The interaction is
+
+```text
+(correct_rollout - correct_onestep)
+- (constant_rollout - constant_onestep)
+```
+
+A positive interaction means that rollout training helps more when the model
+has the correct gravity input. Report the five downstream-seed results as
+probe/evaluation variability, not as independent world-model training runs.
+
+## Scope
+
+This first run tests prediction only. It does not train a downstream diffusion
+policy. The paper gives the policy its own gravity input, which would add a
+second route for the physical parameter to affect control.
